t matrix quantum group the quantum permutation group and write . ... Flat matrix models for quantum permutation groups\n## References\n\n  1. [1]\n\nT. Banica\n\nHomogeneous quantum groups and their easiness level\n\narXiv:1806.06368 (2018)\n\nKyoto J. Math., in press\n\nGoogle Scholar\n\n  2. [2]\n\nT. Banica, J. Bichon\n\nHopf images and inner faithful representations\n\nGlasg. Math. J., 52 (2010), pp. 677-703\n\nView in ScopusGoogle Scholar\n\n  3. [3]\n\nM. Brannan, A. Chirvasitu, A. Freslon\n\nTopological generation and matrix models for quantum reflection groups\n\narXiv:1808.08611 (2018)\n\nGoogle Scholar\n\n  4. [4]\n\nT. Banica, I. Nechita\n\nFlat matrix models for quantum permutation groups\n\nAdv. Appl. Math., 83 (2017), pp. 24-46\n\nView PDFView articleView in ScopusGoogle Scholar\n\n  5. [5]\n\nT. Banica, R. Speicher\n\nLiberation of orthogonal Lie groups\n--------------------------------------------------------------------------------\nTopological generation and matrix models for quantum reflection groups - ScienceDirect (https://doi.org/10.1016/j.aim.2020.106982)\nciteturn8search5 [wordlim: 200] Published: 6.6 years ago; Crawled: last week; The other major set of results in this paper pertain to the quantum permutation groups 𝑆+𝑁. ... While our residual finiteness results ensure the existence of enough finite-dimensional ⁎-representations to separate points in the group algebras 𝒜⁡(𝑆+𝑁)= ℂ⁢̂𝑆+𝑁, it is often desirable to have a single representation 𝜋 :𝒜⁡(𝑆+𝑁) →𝐵, where B is some “nice” model C^{⁎}-algebra (e.g. finite-dimensional, or of the form 𝑀𝑁⁡(𝐶⁡(𝑋))) which encodes enough information about 𝑆+𝑁 so as to generate an asymptotically faithful sequence of finite-dimensional representations (𝜋𝑘)𝑘∈ℕ of 𝒜⁡(𝑆+𝑁). ... Such quantum permutations are typically called “flat” matrix models for 𝑆+𝑁, and the collection 𝑋𝑁 ⊂𝑀𝑁⁡(𝑀𝑁⁡(ℂ)) of all such flat quantum permutations forms a compact space.\nIn the above, Δ :𝒜⁡(𝑆+𝑁) →𝒜⁡(𝑆+𝑁) ⊗𝒜⁡(𝑆+𝑁) denotes the coproduct and Δ(𝑘)= (id⊗Δ(𝑘−1))∘Δ for all 𝑘 ≥2. In particular, this means that 𝒜⁡(𝑆+𝑁) faithfully embeds into a C^{⁎}-ultraproduct of the sequence of algebras (𝐵⊗𝑘)𝑘∈ℕ. One particular “minimal” representation of 𝒜⁡(𝑆+𝑁) that has been conjectured to be inner faithful is Banica-Nechita's universal flat representation [12], [7]. Here, the idea is to consider the simplest examples of non-classical quantum permutations: 𝑁 ×𝑁 bistochastic matrices 𝑃= (𝑃𝑖⁢𝑗)𝑖,𝑗 whose entries are rank-one projections in 𝑃𝑖⁢𝑗 ∈𝑀𝑁⁡(ℂ). Such quantum permutations are typically called “flat” matrix models for 𝑆+𝑁, and the collection 𝑋𝑁 ⊂𝑀𝑁⁡(𝑀𝑁⁡(ℂ)) of all such flat quantum permutations forms a compact space. Banica's universal flat representation then takes the form 𝜋:𝒜⁡(𝑆+𝑁)→𝐶⁡(𝑋𝑁,𝑀𝑁)=𝑀𝑁⁡(𝐶⁡(𝑋𝑁)),𝜋⁡(𝑢𝑖⁢𝑗)={𝑋𝑁∋𝑃↦𝑃𝑖⁢𝑗}. In this paper, we use modifications of our topological generation results to verify the conjectured inner faithfulness of the universal flat representation for almost all values of N (cf. Corollary 4.9).\n\nTheorem 1.3\n\nFor all 𝑁 ≤5 and 𝑁 ≥10, the universal flat matrix model 𝜋 :𝒜⁡(𝑆+𝑁) →𝑀𝑁⁡(𝐶⁡(𝑋𝑁)) is inner faithful.\n\nIntuitively this result says that even though 𝒜⁡(𝑆+𝑁) is a rather unwieldy object, the relatively simple class of 𝑁 ×𝑁 flat quantum permutations is sufficiently rich to fully recover this algebra.\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------------------\nGeneral Compact Quantum Groups, a Tutorial (https://citeseerx.ist.psu.edu/document?doi=a3faef568fc8b9490db7b09e4d8f4a5e3b78ab31&repid=rep1&type=pdf)\nciteturn8search15 [wordlim: 200] Published: 8 months ago; arXiv:hep-th/9401114v1  24 Jan 1994\nIt is the purpose of this paper to give a tutorial introduction to Hopf algebras ... European School of Group Theory 1993, Trento, Italy. ... to the special compact matrix quantum groups most commonly studied nowadays.\nGeneral Compact Quantum Groups, a Tutorial\nTOM H. KOORNWINDER\nAbstract\nA tutorial introduction is given to general Hopf algebras and to general com-\npact quantum groups. In the deﬁnition and further treatment of compact quantum groups\nC*-algebras are avoided. Contact with Woronowicz’s compact matrix quantum groups is\nmade at a later stage.\n0. Introduction\narXiv:hep-th/9401114v1  24 Jan 1994\nIt is the purpose of this paper to give a tutorial introduction to Hopf algebras\nand general compact quantum groups. An earlier version of the paper was part of\nthe lecture notes of my course on “Quantum groups and q-special functions” at the\nEuropean School of Group Theory 1993, Trento, Italy. Eventually, this paper may\nappear as part of a book containing the notes of the main courses at this School.\nFor the reader who is new to this subject, it is crucial that he complements\nreading of the present paper with the study of papers dealing with SUq(2) and other\nspecial quantum groups.\nI now describe the various sections in some more detail. --------------------------------------------------------------------------------\nQuantum automorphism groups (https://d-nb.info/1216104816/34)\nciteturn8search16 [wordlim: 200] Published: 6.3 years ago; includes a short introduction to compact matrix quantum groups, which generalize ... A compact matrix quantum group G is a pair (C(G), u), where\nChapter 1\nPreliminaries\nIn this chapter, we review basic notions and deﬁnitions we need in this thesis. This\nincludes a short introduction to compact matrix quantum groups, which generalize\ncompact groups G ⊆GLn(C). Then, we discuss the quantum symmetric group S+\nn ,\nwhich constitutes the quantum analogue of the symmetric group Sn. In Section 1.2\nwe review basic deﬁnitions and properties of ﬁnite graphs. Note that the automor-\nphism group of a graph is the classical counterpart of the quantum automorphism\ngroup. The latter is important for this work and will be introduced in Chapter 2.\n1.1\nCompact matrix quantum groups\nWe start with compact matrix quantum groups which were deﬁned by Woronowicz\n[60, 62] in 1987. See [42, 54] for recent books on compact quantum groups.\nDeﬁnition 1.1.1. A compact matrix quantum group G is a pair (C(G), u), where\nC(G) is a unital (not necessarily commutative) C∗-algebra which is generated by uij,\n1 ≤i, j ≤n, the entries of a matrix u ∈Mn(C(G)). Moreover, the *-homomorphism\n∆: C(G) →C(G)⊗C(G), uij 7→Pn\nk=1 uik ⊗ukj must exist, and u and its transpose\nut must be invertible. The matrix u is usually called fundamental representation.\nRemark 1.1.2. In the previous deﬁnition, the symbol ⊗denotes the minimal tensor\nproduct of C∗-algebras. Unless stated otherwise, we write A ⊗B for the minimal\ntensor product of the C∗-algebras A and B, throughout this thesis.\nWe now introduce the notion of a quantum subgroup.\nDeﬁnition 1.1.3. Let (C(G), u) and (C(H), v) be compact matrix quantum groups.\n(i) We say that (C(G), u) is a quantum subgroup of (C(H), v), if there is a sur-\njective *-homomorphism ϕ : C(H) →C(G) such that ∆G ◦ϕ = (ϕ ⊗ϕ) ◦∆H.\n11--------------------------------------------------------------------------------\nTopological generation and matrix models for quantum reflection groups (https://arxiv.org/abs/1808.08611)\nciteturn8academia17 [wordlim: 200] Published: 8.2 years ago; We establish several new topological generation results for the quantum permutation groups $S^+_N$ and the quantum reflection groups $H^{s+}_N$. ... In particular, all of these quantum groups have residually finite discrete duals (and are, in particular, hyperlinear), and certain \"flat\" matrix models for $S_N^+$ are inner faithful.\nTitle: Topological generation and matrix models for quantum reflection groups\nAuthors: Michael Brannan, Alexandru Chirvasitu, Amaury Freslon\nDate: Sun Aug 26 19:16:52 2018\n\nWe establish several new topological generation results for the quantum permutation groups $S^+_N$ and the quantum reflection groups $H^{s+}_N$. We use these results to show that these quantum groups admit sufficiently many \"matrix models\". In